# Execution-lane credentials (issues #172, #226). Which variables a pass needs
# is each lane's declaration in lanes.yaml (`auth:`, names only); set the ones
# for the lanes you want available. A lane whose variables are absent is
# reported as unavailable at boot, lane by lane, and refuses to start a pass.
# Every credential reaches an agent container exec-scoped, carried in by the
# lane's harness adapter; nothing is mounted from the host (issue #28).
# The shipped file's lanes, as a worked example (harness adapter in brackets):
#   claude-subscription (claude-code; preferred default) — CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN,
#     a token from `claude setup-token`. Production stores it in Doppler
#     (interlude/prd).
#   anthropic-api (claude-code) — ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   openrouter, openrouter-glm (claude-code) — OPENROUTER_API_KEY
#   opencode-openrouter-glm (opencode) — OPENROUTER_API_KEY, the same key
#   codex-subscription (codex) — CODEX_AUTH_JSON, the contents of the auth.json
#     that `codex login` writes
#   openai-api (codex) — OPENAI_API_KEY
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# CODEX_AUTH_JSON=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Agent model (issues #74, #166). Pin the tier a pass runs at so it is explicit
# and recorded on the run row, instead of silently following the harness's own
# default. Prefer a tier — heavy | standard | light (opus | sonnet | haiku still
# work as aliases) — over a raw model id: a tier survives a change of provider.
# AGENT_MODEL is the base — implement, interactive and repair passes use it
# (a repair runs at the run's own tier, issue #211, and falls back to this with
# none), and triage falls back to it. Review is *derived* (issue #201): it runs
# one rung above the tier the run's implement pass ran at, so a tier in
# AGENT_MODEL_REVIEW is a ceiling on that, not the tier itself; a review with
# nothing to derive from resolves as any other pass does — AGENT_MODEL_REVIEW,
# else AGENT_MODEL, which never caps it. Leave AGENT_MODEL_REVIEW unset and the
# derivation runs free. Leave all three unset to keep the pre-#74 behaviour for
# everything else (no model named; the lane's harness resolves its own default,
# a priced lane resolves `standard`). What a tier means as a model is the
# lane's `models` map in lanes.yaml. These are the *fall-through*: Settings ->
# Models sets a per-pass-kind tier from the UI, and a row left on `environment`
# reads the variable below.
# AGENT_MODEL=heavy
# AGENT_MODEL_REVIEW=standard
# AGENT_MODEL_TRIAGE=light

# Agent reasoning effort (issue #81) — the other half of the cost/quality dial
# alongside the model. The levels are fleet vocabulary — low | medium | high |
# xhigh | max — and each lane's harness adapter maps a level onto its own dial,
# or passes no flag where it has no equivalent. AGENT_EFFORT is the base —
# implement, repair and interactive passes use it. Review and triage are
# read-heavy and cheaper-effort candidates: give them their own override, else
# they fall back to AGENT_EFFORT. Leave all three unset to keep each harness's
# own default. A ticket's `effort:` directive (Workflow section) may pin a work
# pass to one of the levels above.
# AGENT_EFFORT=high
# AGENT_EFFORT_REVIEW=medium
# AGENT_EFFORT_TRIAGE=low

# Optional
GIT_USER_NAME=Interlude Agent
GIT_USER_EMAIL=agent@interlude.dev
KEEP_CONTAINERS=false

# Agent limits. MAX_BUDGET_USD changed meaning in Phase 5 (issue #18): it is
# the default budget per autonomous *attempt* (one claim, fresh container) —
# raisable per ticket via a `budget:` directive up to a hard $75 ceiling —
# and interactive tasks inherit the same per-task default. It was a $5
# per-task cap before Phase 5; the default deliberately rose with the change.
# Review passes carry their own ~$5 allowance. MAX_TURNS is per exec; a
# ticket's `max-turns:` directive may raise an attempt's to at most 100.
MAX_TURNS=50
MAX_BUDGET_USD=20.00

# Real money (issue #174). The cash the fleet may spend in one local day
# through a *metered* execution lane — a lane in lanes.yaml whose `billing` is
# `metered`, whether it is primary, an overflow target or reached by failover.
# Subscription work never counts against it: this number measures money, not
# quota, which is why it is a separate (and far smaller) ceiling from the
# $500/day autonomous cap. Reaching it pauses new autonomous pickup until local
# midnight, and no autonomous pass starts on a metered lane until the day's
# spend has been confirmed once on the settings screen. In-flight runs and
# interactive sessions are never held — their spend still counts. Settable from
# that screen too, up to a hard $100 code ceiling; unset, it falls through to
# this default.
METERED_DAILY_CAP_USD=20.00

# Capacity — slots derive at boot from the Docker daemon's CPU/memory;
# override only when the derivation is wrong for a workload
# CAPACITY_SLOTS=2
# AGENT_MEMORY_MB=1200  # per-agent hard memory cap; also the slot divisor

# Deployment (production only)
# DATABASE_URL=/data/interlude.db  # set in docker-compose.yml environment block; override here only if needed
# DOMAIN — production's single source of truth is Doppler (interlude/prd): the
#   app reads it at boot via `doppler run`; the deploy renders caddy.env from the
#   same config for the reverse proxy. Never hand-set DOMAIN in the VPS .env
#   (issue #25). Set it here only for local (non-Doppler) subdomain testing.
# DOMAIN=interlude.dev

# GitHub App — REQUIRED for git (clone/push) and for issue sync + PR creation.
# The agent authenticates git using a short-lived App installation token; there is no PAT to rotate.
GITHUB_APP_ID=123456
GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\n..."
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
GITHUB_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=12345678

# Discord Bot (optional — enables task notifications + dispatch from Discord)
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=your-bot-token
# DISCORD_APPLICATION_ID=your-application-id
# DISCORD_GUILD_ID=your-guild-id  # server id, used to deep-link into project channels
# DISCORD_FLEET_CHANNEL_ID — fleet events (slot saturation, digest) and the
#   fallback channel for a blocked agent's question when its project has no
#   linked channel. Without it, those land only on the dashboard / task chat.
# DISCORD_FLEET_CHANNEL_ID=your-channel-id

# Autonomy (Phase 5 — autonomous ticket-loop). Off by default; see docs/runbook.md.
# Global kill switch: BOTH this and a project's own toggle must be on for pickup.
# Read once at boot, so a change needs an orchestrator restart.
# AUTONOMY_ENABLED=false
# Extra GitHub logins (comma-separated) allowed to author claimable issues.
# The repo owner is always allowed; this is defence in depth behind the
# human-only ready-for-agent labelling rule.
# AUTONOMY_ALLOWED_AUTHORS=someone,another
# Reviewer machine-account PAT — the orchestrator posts PR reviews with it and
# it NEVER enters an agent container. Canonical home is Doppler platform/prd,
# mirrored into interlude/prd (rotation must update both).
# REVIEWER_GH_TOKEN=github_pat_...

# Quota admission gate (issue #171): above this much of the account's quota
# window — or once the account is already being rejected — no NEW ticket is
# claimed and no triage pass is started. Work already in flight finishes and
# parked runs still resume; the kill switch and each project's toggle still
# come first. Settable at runtime in Settings -> Quota, which overrides this.
# One of 50, 70, 80, 85, 90, 95, 100 (100 = gate only on an outright
# rejection); a blank/unrecognised value keeps the default of 90.
# QUOTA_PICKUP_THRESHOLD_PERCENT=90

# Quota resume bound (issue #169): how many times ONE attempt may be paused by
# the account's quota and resumed before its ticket is routed to a human the
# way an exhausted one is. A pause spends no attempt and no money, so this
# bounds how long a ticket may sit crossing windows, not what it costs. 0 sends
# a quota pause straight to a human. Settable at runtime in Settings -> Quota,
# which overrides this. One of 0-5; a blank/unrecognised value keeps the
# default of 3.
# MAX_RESUMES_PER_ATTEMPT=3

# Fleet-health watchdog thresholds (issue #126), in MINUTES. Each surfaces a
# needs-you card + one Discord fleet ping when a stall lasts past the threshold.
# Defaults shown; a blank/invalid value keeps the default.
# OWED_REVIEW_STALL_MINUTES=30      # a reviewing/gated run whose review never started
# PICKUP_WEDGED_MINUTES=3           # a free slot but claimable work not dispatching
# QUEUE_HEARTBEAT_STALE_MINUTES=2   # the 2s queue poll loop stopped making progress
